{
  "case_id": "2020-19-71",
  "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Edwin S. Kneedler (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In enacting RFRA, Congress did not subject federal employees throughout the government to a new cause of action for damages in their personal capacity. RFRA's remedy section provides only for appropriate relief against the government. Damages against an individual employee in his personal capacity are not relief against the government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Edwin S. Kneedler (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the principle derives from the point I just made about the Westfall Act and what -- and, more generally, Congress's practice with respect to establishing private damage remedies against federal employees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice THOMAS: Thank you, Chief Justice. Mr. Kneedler, let's assume you take this out of the context of the government and you simply have a private cause of action that provides for appropriate relief. Would money damages be available then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: But, Mr. Kneedler, I'm sorry, I wish to redirect you back to the question, which is, as I understood it, the government agrees that a private contractor can be sued for damages, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. KNEEDLER",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We think all the pertinent factors to look at here strongly indicate that personal damages actions are not available. The text is clear, providing for relief only against the government, which doesn't include monetary damages."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ramzi KASSEM",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "To be sure, such cases are hard to win. Qualified immunity will shield all but those who defy clearly established law. But there is no basis for categorically excluding damages where they are warranted and essential to providing meaningful relief to victims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I -- I have one question about one of your opponent's arguments. Look at the phrase \"person acting under color of law.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, Petitioners argue that you haven't identified any statute in which the phrase \"appropriate relief\" was interpreted to include a damages remedy against federal officials, absent some extra text making the availability of damages explicit. Is that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I'm sorry. Are you taking the position that Congress didn't intend, by using appropriate relief, to include -- before Sossamon -- to include states and their -- their -- their officials? I mean, there were private causes of action under 1983 for damages against the state government and its officials. Why would Congress not have wanted to pierce sovereign immunity with respect to states and their officials back then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-19-71",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b765d6fc7bdd552f34b38a2d2625f9362a12e4797846054be27a42c46426ec28",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b765d6fc7bdd-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Kassem, if I could just follow up on Justice Sotomayor's first question. As I understood your answer, you said well, RFRA is a very special statute, a super-statute. But assume that I don't think that for the moment. Assume that I don't think that RFRA has any special interpretive rules attached to it, that I think it should be interpreted the same way other statutes are."
    },
